147
00:19:17.075 --> 00:19:23.340
<v Daniel>cutting job postings for junior non-AI using roles. Where does that leave the future of work?</v>

148
00:19:24.095 --> 00:19:38.826
<v Maya>It forces us to confront a pretty profound structural question about corporate training and the lifecycle of a career. If agents primarily replace the hiring of juniors, we are effectively removing the bottom rung of the corporate ladder. The entire architecture of the knowledge economy</v>

149
00:19:39.207 --> 00:19:45.912
<v Maya>relies on a flow of entry-level workers making mistakes, learning the institutional ropes, and gradually maturing into senior management.</v>

150
00:19:46.282 --> 00:19:58.357
<v Daniel>Which loops us right back to the apprentice and the master carpenter. If you never hire the apprentice to sweep the floor and carve the simple table legs, where do the master carpenters of 2036 even come from?</v>

151
00:19:58.637 --> 00:19:59.017
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

152
00:19:59.796 --> 00:20:14.742
<v Daniel>For a business analytics student listening to this right now, the most actionable piece of advice comes straight from the data anomaly we discussed earlier. Postings expecting AI tool use did not fall. Your value in the marketplace is no longer</v>

153
00:20:14.802 --> 00:20:24.646
<v Daniel>derived from your ability to execute routine tasks. No, it's not. Your value is entirely dependent on your ability to orchestrate and audit the digital agents that execute those tasks.</v>

154
00:20:25.371 --> 00:20:39.731
<v Maya>And we must also remember that this lead paper is a newly posted working paper. The science is unfolding in real time, and it has not yet undergone formal peer review. We will need years of ex-post employment data to finally settle the tension we explored earlier.</v>

155
00:20:40.066 --> 00:20:41.527
<v Daniel>Whether it's hype or reality.</v>

156
00:20:41.687 --> 00:20:55.972
<v Maya>Right. Is this a true permanent technological substitution of human labor or is it just an ongoing shock to corporate expectations? CEOs might be pausing their entry level hiring today because they think the agents can handle the workload only to realize in two years that they</v>

157
00:20:56.192 --> 00:20:58.853
<v Maya>desperately still need human intuition at the ground level.</v>

158
00:20:59.469 --> 00:21:14.335
<v Daniel>Which leaves you with a deeply provocative thought to mull over. We always assumed that automation would come for the physical, repetitive labor first. But it turns out the exact environments where tacit human knowledge, intuition, and mentorship are</v>

159
00:21:14.535 --> 00:21:20.837
<v Daniel>forged, the entry-level white-collar jobs, are the ones being most aggressively absorbed by agents.</v>

160
00:21:21.505 --> 00:21:22.267
<v Maya>It's ironic, really.</v>

161
00:21:22.467 --> 00:21:37.398
<v Daniel>It is. Are we inadvertently dismantling our own training grounds? Will the future workplace be governed by a generation of senior managers who never actually had to learn the ground level ropes themselves? Keep exploring that</v>

162
00:21:37.438 --> 00:21:40.424
<v Daniel>thought and keep learning. We'll catch you on the next deep dive.</v>
